WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.370
- [Narrator] Over the past 20 years,

00:00:01.370 --> 00:00:03.930
the US has borrowed more and more money.

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:06.140
As a comparison to the share of GDP,

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:08.050
it's around 100%,

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:10.090
the highest since World War II.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.700
The biggest recent hike happened in 2020

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.850
when the coronavirus pandemic hit the US

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:17.030
and crippled the nation's economy.

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:18.290
- Congress finally cleared

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:21.870
a $900 billion coronavirus relief package

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:23.300
just before midnight last night

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.910
and with it a $1.4 trillion bill

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:28.080
that funds the government
through the fiscal year.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.070
- [Narrator] The question of whether

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:30.320
it's economically feasible

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:32.290
to keep adding to this mountain of debt

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:33.940
has long vexed Congress,

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:35.660
which is in charge of the debt ceiling,

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:38.660
the law that decides how much
the US is allowed to borrow.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:42.770
- We Democrats supported
lifting the debt ceiling

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:45.110
because it's the responsible thing to do.

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:46.820
- I implore them one more time

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:48.200
not to play Russian roulette

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:49.670
with the American economy.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:50.820
- [Narrator] As lawmakers prepare

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.180
to likely raise the ceiling

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:53.900
again in early December,

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.870
here's a look at how the US's debt works

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:00.400
and why economists say the
US can keep on borrowing,

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.080
at least to a point,

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:05.010
provided, of course, Congress
raises the debt limit.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:06.710
- There are some limitations though

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:08.850
on how much they can ultimately borrow,

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.600
and those have to do with
inflation and interest rates.

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:14.610
- [Narrator] Here's what happens
when the government needs

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:17.360
to borrow more money to may
for its economic agenda.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:19.420
First, Congress passes laws

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:21.980
to determine spending and taxes.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:23.690
If the government spends so much

00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:26.140
that spending exceeds taxes,

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.670
the Treasury must finance
the deficit by borrowing,

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:30.820
issuing Treasury bonds and bills.

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:33.560
- When Treasury issues a Treasury bond

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:34.440
or a Treasury bill,

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.320
this is basically an IOU.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.620
An investor gives the Treasury some money

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:39.453
with an expectation

00:01:39.453 --> 00:01:41.370
that they'll get the
money back with interest.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:43.550
- [Narrator] Greg Ip, who
has covered the US economy

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:44.460
for The Wall Street Journal

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.290
for nearly two decades says

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:49.550
that people view Treasury bonds
as a safe option for saving.

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:52.120
So when the Treasury
sells bonds to investors,

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:53.520
two things happen.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.370
First, the government gets
money to pay its bills,

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:59.580
and second, it agrees to
pay investors interest.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.170
- So when the government issues debt,

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.740
they will use that money
to meet all the obligations

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:05.240
the federal government has.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:06.870
They may use it to send

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.240
Social Security benefits
checks to recipients.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:12.110
They may use it to pay the
salaries of the military

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:14.450
or the civilians in the
federal civil service.

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:16.580
And they may use it to pay the interest

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:18.310
on the debt they've borrowed in the past.

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:21.100
If interest rates are
zero, then in theory,

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:23.340
the government could
borrow an infinite amount

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.570
because it will never have
to pay any interest on it.

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:28.240
Conversely, if interest
rates are extremely high,

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.650
that really limits how much
the government can borrow

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.960
because the interest
bill would be so large.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:36.220
- [Narrator] Today interest
rates are at historic lows,

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:38.080
which economists say means the government

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:39.390
can keep borrowing.

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:41.680
But the Treasury is
competing for savers' money

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.160
with other borrowers,

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:46.330
like households, companies
and other governments.

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:48.720
If those other people
decide to borrow more,

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.160
the Treasury would have to
offer a higher interest rate

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:52.990
to attract those savings.

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:54.270
- So if interest rates go up a lot,

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:56.050
that's going to significantly
increase the bill

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:57.840
that the government pays
for interest on its debt,

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:00.188
in which case, one of three
things have to happen.

00:03:00.188 --> 00:03:02.280
Either they have to raise taxes

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:03.720
to pay that added interest.

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.680
They have to cut other types of spending,

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:07.150
which nobody likes.

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:09.160
Or it has to borrow even more.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:10.850
And then you get this cycle

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:13.130
where high interest
forces you to borrow more,

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:13.990
which raises the debt,

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:15.750
which forces interest rates even higher,

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:16.990
and then you have to borrow even more.

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:19.970
And that's a very dangerous
cycle to get into.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:22.390
- [Narrator] But there's
another limitation too.

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:23.770
If Congress puts too much

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:25.895
of the borrowed money
back into the economy

00:03:25.895 --> 00:03:28.100
through things like stimulus checks,

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:29.360
that can cause inflation.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:30.480
- If it borrows that money

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:31.530
and then goes and spends it,

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:34.690
and that spending exceeds
the economy's ability

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:37.130
to produce the goods and services needed

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:38.580
to satisfy that spending,

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:39.820
the result of too much spending

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:42.610
chasing too few goods,
and you get inflation.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:45.240
And when inflation goes
up, interest rates go up,

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:46.580
and you're back into that problem

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:48.290
of the high interest bill

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:49.900
making the debt unsupportable.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.880
- [Narrator] Inflation
was 5.3% in the 12 months

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:56.180
through August, close to
the highest in 12 years.

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:59.600
But Ip says this is largest a
different type of inflation.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:02.640
- This seems to reflect
mostly temporary factors.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:03.910
We're coming out of this pandemic,

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:05.890
so there's suddenly this surge in demand,

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:07.620
and a lot of the restraints on the supply

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:08.960
of a lot of products.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.740
Hopefully these transitory shortages

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:12.710
will go away in a year or two

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:14.365
and inflation will fall back.

00:04:14.365 --> 00:04:16.310
The kind of inflation you worry about

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:18.700
is when you have persistent
levels of spending

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:23.070
that constantly exceed the
economy's fundamental capacity

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.470
to supply stuff.

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:25.730
That's the kind of inflation

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:27.451
that lasts for a long time.

00:04:27.451 --> 00:04:30.000
- [Narrator] So back to Congress' role.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.887
- The debt ceiling needs to be addressed.

00:04:31.887 --> 00:04:35.890
The only question is
who should address it?

00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:38.220
And I hope our friends on
the other side will step up

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:39.210
and take care of it.

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:42.880
- Since 2011, each of the seven times

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:44.850
that the debt limit was addressed,

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:48.190
Congress did so on a bipartisan basis.

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:50.040
- [Narrator] Congress
decides on the debt limit.

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:51.410
When the Treasury has reached it,

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:53.360
it can't issue more until Congress votes

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:54.730
to raise the debt limit.

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:57.540
That's even if Congress
already passed a budget

00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:59.150
that requires borrowing.

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:00.840
This has led some to wonder

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:02.410
why can't we just print off the money

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:04.280
to pay the mountain of debt?

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:06.150
- If the Fed prints too
much money this way,

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:06.983
you're back into that problem

00:05:06.983 --> 00:05:09.293
of the Treasury spending that money

00:05:09.293 --> 00:05:12.480
and causing inflation and cause
the interest rates to go up.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:13.313
- [Narrator] With the option

00:05:13.313 --> 00:05:15.130
of printing money off the table,

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:17.260
it will ultimately come down to Congress

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:19.040
to raise the debt limit in December

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:21.078
to avoid a default on the debt.

00:05:21.078 --> 00:05:23.745
(pensive music)

